******************************************************************2722 1451   58
**                                                              **2722 1451   59
** This is  a completly new evaluation for Co58 in the thermal  **2722 1451   60
   resonance, unresolved and fast regions.                        2722 1451   61
                                                                  2722 1451   62
 THERMAL REGION                                                   2722 1451   63
                                                                  2722 1451   64
 Evaluation May 14, 2011 by S.F Mughabghab and B. Pritychenko,BNL 2722 1451   65
                                                                  2722 1451   66
 The only available neutronic information for Co-58 is            2722 1451   67
 the thermal neutron capture cross section and the capture        2722 1451   68
 resonance integral [Mu06]. Since the (n,p) threshold is 3.090 MeV2722 1451   69
 below the neutron separation energy of the compound nucleus      2722 1451   70
 Co-59,a knowledge of the thermal (n,p) cross section is required.2722 1451   71
                                                                  2722 1451   72
  To obtain this information, two methods were applied.           2722 1451   73
                                                                  2722 1451   74
  1. The reciprocity theorem and detailed balance were applied    2722 1451   75
     to the reaction Fe58(p,n)Co58.                               2722 1451   76
     The (n,p) values thus obtained in the low keV region         2722 1451   77
     were extrapolated to the thermal energy by the 1/v law.      2722 1451   78
                                                                  2722 1451   79
  2. The ratio of the (n,p) cross section of Co-58 to that of     2722 1451   80
     Ni-59  in the kev region is determined by the EMPIRE code    2722 1451   81
     and then normalized to the thermal (n,p) cross section of    2722 1451   82
     Ni59.                                                        2722 1451   83
                                                                  2722 1451   84
    In spite of the crude assumtions, both methods, surprisingly, 2722 1451   85
    yielded the same thermal (n,p) cross section, 101 b for       2722 1451   86
    Co-58. This value is in disagreement with previous            2722 1451   87
    evaluations giving cross sections over 1700 b.                2722 1451   88
                                                                  2722 1451   89
 RESONANCE PARAMETERS                                           &&2722 1451   90
                                                                &&2722 1451   91
 Evaluation, May 14 2011 by S.F. Mughabghab (BNL)               &&2722 1451   92
                                                                  2722 1451   93
  RESOLVED RESONANCES in MLBW                                     2722 1451   94
                                                                  2722 1451   95
   As a starting point, the thermal capture cross section, as     2722 1451   96
   well as the capture resonance integral were adopted from       2722 1451   97
   Mughabghab[Mu06].                                              2722 1451   98
                                                                  2722 1451   99
   These  two quantities were assumed to be attributed to a low   2722 1451  100
   energy resonance determined at an energy of 10.35 eV.          2722 1451  101
                                                                  2722 1451  102
   If the  thermal (n,p) cross section is also attributed to      2722 1451  103
   this resonance, then the proton width of this resonance is     2722 1451  104
   calculated as  27.28 meV. The  (n,p) cross section             2722 1451  105
   at the peak of this resonance is computed as 2030 b.           2722 1451  106
   A s-wave average captue width of 0.83 eV is calculated on the  2722 1451  107
   basis of the generalized Fermi-liquid model of Mughabghab      2722 1451  108
   and Dunford [Mu00].                                            2722 1451  109
                                                                  2722 1451  110
   An average s-wave resonance spacing of 325 eV was determined   2722 1451  111
   for Co58 with the Gilbert-Cameron relation and associated      2722 1451  112
   paremeters [Mu98].                                             2722 1451  113
                                                                  2722 1451  114
                                                                  2722 1451  115
   Effective scattering radius of 6.73 fm was adopted from        2722 1451  116
    [Mu06] and [Mu10c].                                           2722 1451  117
                                                                  2722 1451  118
   Average radiative widths of 0.83 eV was assumed for s-wave     2722 1451  119
   resonances,                                                    2722 1451  120
                                                                  2722 1451  121
                                                                  2722 1451  122
                                                                  2722 1451  123
  Calculated thermal cross sections & resonance integrals:      &&2722 1451  124
      ---------------------------------------------             &&2722 1451  125
      Reaction       Cross section    Res. integral             &&2722 1451  126
                        (barn)           (barn)                 &&2722 1451  127
                                                                &&2722 1451  128
      Total           2027.4               -                    &&2722 1451  129
      (n,p)            101.0               -                    &&2722 1451  130
      Elastic           71.4               -                    &&2722 1451  131
      Capture         1855.0              6519                  &&2722 1451  132
      ---------------------------------------------             &&2722 1451  133
                                                                &&2722 1451  134
     With these parameters, the Wescott factor for the capture  &&2722 1451  135
     reaction is calculated as g_w = 1.00609.                   &&2722 1451  136
                                                                  2722 1451  137
  UNRESOLVED RESONANCE REGION in 'all energy-dependent parameters'2722 1451  138
                              format (LRF=2)                      2722 1451  139
   Average level spacing was calculated on basis of the Gilbert-  2722 1451  140
     Cameron relation and the value of D for Co59.                2722 1451  141
                                                                  2722 1451  142
   Strength functions for s-, p- and d-wave neutrons were adopted 2722 1451  143
     from  the systematics [Mu06].                                2722 1451  144
                                                                  2722 1451  145
   Average radiative widths for s- and p-wave neutrons  of 0.83 eV2722 1451  146
      and 0.415 eV, respectively were derived from systematics    2722 1451  147
      obtained from fits to  the average capture cross section in 2722 1451  148
      the unresolved energy region [Mu10a] and [Mu10b].           2722 1451  149
      For d-wave neutrons the average capture width,0.590 eV,     2722 1451  150
      is obtained as the geometric mean of the s- and             2722 1451  151
      p-wave components.                                          2722 1451  152
                                                                  2722 1451  153
   Energy-dependence of level spacing was calculated according to 2722 1451  154
     the Gilbert-Cameron level density formula with associated    2722 1451  155
     parameters from Mughabghab [Mu06],[Mu98].                    2722 1451  156
                                                                  2722 1451  157
   Energy-dependence of capture widths is calculated on the       2722 1451  158
    basis of the generalized Fermi liquid model [Mu98] with       2722 1451  159
    associated parameters from [Mu06].                            2722 1451  160
                                                                  2722 1451  161
  Average parameters:                                             2722 1451  162
                S (10**4)     <D> (eV)*   <Gg> (eV)               2722 1451  163
    s-wave      4.0            325          0.830                 2722 1451  164
    p-wave      0.31           175          0.415                 2722 1451  165
    d-wave      4.0            134          0.590                 2722 1451  166
   * Level spacing  and capture widths at the neutron separation  2722 1451  167
     energy of target+n.                                          2722 1451  168
